, or valuation data.\n\n**Debt (n7.r1): open, but only partly.**\n- Norvane priced a C$1.1B bond \"comfortably\" in Q3 2026, so investment-grade access is intact for at least one issuer.\n- Utility bond spreads widened 30bp in Q3 to 165bp, so funding is getting more expensive.\n- Capex plans are rising fast: Pellham's 2027 plan is up 18% and Norvane's is up 12%. Debt alone probably won't close that gap.\n\n**Equity (n8.r1): not reliably.**\n- Pellham withdrew a C$2.4B equity offering because demand fell short.\n- Two other utility share offerings were downsized in Q3.\n- Pellham will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and defer two substation programmes \"until financing improves.\" So its capex plan depends on equity funding, and that funding isn't available.\n\n**Net view:**\n- **Norvane-type issuers:** these can probably fund through bonds.\n- **Pellham-type issuers:** these are likely to scale back. I'm inferring that Pellham is the weaker or more equity-reliant name from its actions, because the documents don't give its leverage.\n- **Confidence:** moderate.\n\n**Not available:** issuance volumes beyond the Norvane deal, 2027 maturity walls, credit metrics such as FFO/debt and ratings headroom, valuation multiples, and dilution estimates. If Pellham is near its rating thresholds, more debt may not be an option either.","ttft_ms":4887,"type":"result","duration_ms":39821,"uuid":"9fdfbffb-597a-4917-90ee-887cc03974fb","ttft_stream_ms":4394,"time_to_request_ms":154,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":12,"system_prompt":11,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":8,"input_attachments":20,"input_hooks":6,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":13,"tool_pool_refresh":1,"init_message":17,"init_frame_send":5,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":3,"message_prep":0,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":10,"request_announcements":15,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":2,"tool_schema_build":4,"message_normalization":3,"client_creation":6,"request_build":15},"turn_start_resume_kin